My Data Analytics Project 2: Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)

About My Project
My goal for this project was to interrogate the data to uncover hidden patterns, analyze sales distributions, track down key revenue drivers, and present actionable insights that help the business grow.


Detailed Step-by-Step Data Analysis Process

1. Descriptive Statistics & Center of Gravity
I calculated the baseline metrics across the dataset to understand our numbers. I checked the total order volumes, the arithmetic mean (average sales values), and the median (middle point) to see how our typical sales perform without extreme figures skewing the view.

2. Product Performance Analysis
I grouped the dataset by product types to see exactly which items generate the highest customer demand and the most revenue. 
* High Revenue Drivers: Chairs and Printers are our leading revenue generators, each bringing in over $195,000.
* High Volume Items: Chairs (562 items sold) and Printers (542 items sold) are also our fastest-moving stock pieces.
* Lowest Performer: Phones brought in the lowest total revenue ($151,722) and lowest volume (411 items sold).

3. Trend Assessment & The "June Spike"
By tracking sales chronologically across month-by-month periods, I discovered a major seasonal sales trend. Our highest revenue period across the entire dataset occurred in June 2024, generating a massive $68,068 in a single month. This points to a clear mid-year seasonal demand spike.

4. Handling Missing Data (The Coupon Code Column)
During my analysis, I found that the only column containing missing data was the Coupon Code section. These blank spots represented transactions where customers did not use a discount. To clean the view for reporting, I updated these blanks to read "No Coupon" or "None" so they group correctly.


Dashboard Metrics & Key Performance Indicators (KPIs)
To translate these raw numbers into a clear executive view, my analysis sets up the structure for a business dashboard tracking four vital metrics:
* Total Sales Revenue: The overall financial health of our catalog.
* Average Order Value (AOV): How much a customer spends on average per checkout transaction.
* Order Volume by Product: A clear bar chart breakdown showing item popularity.
* Order Status Distribution: A tracking metric showing what percentage of orders are Shipped, Pending, or Cancelled.


Key Business Insights
* Product Concentration: Chairs and Printers are the backbone of our sales funnel. They drive both the highest transaction volumes and financial totals simultaneously.
* Seasonality: The "June Spike" is a recurring business signal. Sales increase drastically mid-year, which likely matches corporate restocking cycles or mid-year consumer sales.
* Phone Underperformance: Phones are lagging significantly behind all other electronics (Laptops and Monitors) in both items sold and revenue generated.



Actionable Business Recommendations
* Capitalize on Seasonality: Increase marketing budgets and run targeted email campaigns in May to fully capture the massive surge of buyers before the June sales spike hits.
* Stock Management: Keep inventory levels high for Chairs and Printers, especially leading into the summer months, to prevent running out of stock on our highest-value drivers.
* Re-evaluate Phone Strategy: Since Phones are our lowest-performing product category, the marketing team should introduce bundle offers (e.g., buy a Laptop, get a discount on a Phone) or offer specific coupon codes to boost phone sales volume.



How My Excel Workbook Is Organized
To make grading easy for my facilitator, the updated Excel workbook Dataset for Data Analytics Cleaned.xlsx is organized into clear sheets:
* Cleaned data: The final, pristine dataset containing all verified rows.
* Charts summary & Descriptive Statistics: The dedicated analytical pages containing my summary formulas, product breakdowns, and trend calculations.
